# JSON module specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented mm.json module and the json tool
over it: the status vocabulary, the scanner, the value model with parse and
write, the strict readings the module takes where RFC 8259 leaves a choice,
and what its tests pin. docs/modules-c++20.mdy owns the language rules the
module is written to; docs/modules-execution.mdy owns the rule that an
output changes only on Ok, which every call here keeps; docs/modules-build.mdy
owns how the build reads compile_commands.json, which it does through this
module.

mm.json is a core module with no use: edge. It reads and writes RFC 8259
JSON without exceptions, without templates of its own, and -- in the scanner
-- without allocation, so that the same module serves the host tools and a
target application holding a document in a fixed buffer.

# Status

Opening fence
enum class Status {
    Ok, Malformed, Truncated, TooDeep, BadEscape, BadUnicode, BadNumber,
    DuplicateKey, Overflow,
};

struct Issue {
    std::size_t offset;          // byte offset of the fault
    unsigned int line;           // counted from one
    unsigned int column;         // counted from one, in bytes
    Status status;
    std::string_view description;   // fixed text for the status
};

[[nodiscard]] constexpr std::string_view describe(Status);
Closing fence

Ok is the only success. Malformed is a character the grammar does not allow
where it stands; Truncated is input that ended inside a value or before one;
TooDeep is nesting past maximum_depth; BadEscape is an escape the grammar
does not name; BadUnicode is an unpaired surrogate escape or malformed UTF-8
in a string; BadNumber is a number the type cannot hold; DuplicateKey is an
object key repeated under the rejecting option; Overflow is an output span
too small for a decoded string. An Issue says where and what; its
description is describe(status) and is never built at runtime.

# The scanner

Opening fence
enum class Token { ObjectBegin, ObjectEnd, ArrayBegin, ArrayEnd, Key, String,
                   Integer, Number, True, False, Null, End };

struct Event { Token token; std::size_t offset; std::size_t length;
               unsigned int depth; };

inline constexpr unsigned int maximum_depth = 64;

class Scanner {
public:
    explicit Scanner(std::string_view text);
    [[nodiscard]] Status next(Event& event);
    [[nodiscard]] const Issue& issue() const;
    [[nodiscard]] Status skip();
};

struct Decoded { Status status; std::size_t count; };
[[nodiscard]] Decoded unescape(std::string_view escaped, std::span<char> out);
[[nodiscard]] Status integer(std::string_view digits, long long& value);
[[nodiscard]] Status number(std::string_view digits, double& value);
Closing fence

Scanner is a pull reader over a string view the caller owns. next answers
one token per call and End after a complete document; event changes only
on Ok, and on any other answer issue() names the fault, the scanner stays at
it, and every later call answers the same status. A String or Key event
spans the bytes between the quotes, still escaped; a number event spans its
digits. depth is the number of containers enclosing the token.

The scanner allocates nothing and recurses nowhere. Its state is the view,
a cursor with its line and column, a depth, three sixty-four bit sets -- one
bit per open level for whether it is an object, whether it holds a value so
a comma or a close comes next, and whether it has read a key and awaits its
colon and value -- and the last issue. A comma is not a token: next
consumes it with the item that follows, so a trailing comma fails at the
bracket. maximum_depth is the width of the sets, and a sixty-fifth opening
bracket answers TooDeep.

next implements RFC 8259 strictly: the four whitespace characters; the
number production, so that -0, 1e5, and 1E+5 are numbers and 01, .5, 1.,
+1, NaN, and Infinity are Malformed; one top-level value, with a second
Malformed; a leading UTF-8 byte order mark skipped. A number with no
fraction and no exponent is an Integer token, any other a Number. Inside a
string it validates as it passes: a raw control character is Malformed; an
escape the grammar does not name is BadEscape; UTF-8 that is not well-formed
-- a bad lead byte, a missing continuation, an overlong form, an encoded
surrogate, a code point past U+10FFFF -- is BadUnicode; and surrogate
escapes are paired as they are met, so an unpaired high or low surrogate is
BadUnicode at the string, before any caller decodes it. skip, parse, and a
scan-only reader therefore refuse the same documents.

skip walks past the value the last token began: the contents of a container
whose opening was just returned, or the value of a key just returned; after
a scalar it does nothing.

unescape decodes a String or Key event's bytes into out: every escape the
grammar names, including \uXXXX and surrogate pairs, to UTF-8. It validates
and counts first and writes second, so out changes only on Ok. Its answer
carries the byte count: what was written on Ok, what would have been needed
on Overflow, so a caller can size a buffer and try again, and zero otherwise.
integer answers BadNumber for a fraction, an exponent, or a value outside
long long; number answers BadNumber for a magnitude beyond double and zero,
with the number's sign, for a magnitude beneath it. Each output changes only
on Ok.

# The value

Opening fence
enum class Type { Null, Boolean, Integer, Number, String, Array, Object };

class Value {
public:
    Value();                                   // Null
    explicit Value(bool);
    explicit Value(long long);
    explicit Value(double);
    explicit Value(std::string_view);
    static Value array();
    static Value object();
    [[nodiscard]] Type type() const;
    [[nodiscard]] bool is_null() const;
    [[nodiscard]] bool boolean(bool& out) const;
    [[nodiscard]] bool integer(long long& out) const;
    [[nodiscard]] bool number(double& out) const;      // true for Integer too
    [[nodiscard]] std::string_view string() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::span<const Value> items() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::span<const Member> members() const;
    [[nodiscard]] const Value* find(std::string_view key) const;
    bool push(Value item);                             // Array only
    bool set(std::string_view key, Value value);       // Object only
    [[nodiscard]] bool equals(const Value& other) const;
};

struct Member { std::string key; Value value; };

enum class Duplicates { Reject, KeepFirst, KeepLast };
enum class WideIntegers { Reject, Approximate };
struct ParseOptions { Duplicates duplicates; WideIntegers wide_integers; };
struct Outcome { Status status; Issue issue; };

[[nodiscard]] Outcome parse(std::string_view text, Value& out,
                            ParseOptions options = {});

enum class Layout { Compact, Indented };
[[nodiscard]] Outcome write(const Value& value, std::string& out,
                            Layout layout = Layout::Compact);
Closing fence

Value owns its string and its children in standard containers, one field per
type rather than a variant. Members keep document order, so a document
round-trips as it was written; find is a linear scan answering the first
match. The accessors that answer a value answer false and leave the output
alone for the wrong type; the string and span accessors answer empty. push
and set answer false and change nothing on the wrong type; set replaces an
existing key's value in place and appends a new key. equals compares by
value, with -0.0 and 0.0 unequal.

Integer is kept distinct from Number: 42 reads and writes as 42, 1.0 as 1.0.
An integer token beyond long long is BadNumber under WideIntegers::Reject,
the default, because converting 9223372036854775809 to a double yields
9223372036854775808 and a reader that changed a digit of an identifier is
worse than one that refused it; under Approximate it is a Number holding the
nearest double. An integer -0 is 0. A magnitude beyond double is BadNumber;
a magnitude beneath it is a Number zero with the number's sign, since that
is what the number denotes to any precision a double has.

parse drives the scanner to completion with an explicit stack, so the
recursion the scanner avoids is not reintroduced, and moves the document
into out only on Ok; on any failure out is as it was, and the Outcome
carries the scanner's Issue or, for DuplicateKey, the offset of the repeated
key's opening quote. Duplicates::Reject is this module's policy: RFC 8259
recommends unique keys and does not require them. KeepFirst keeps the first
member and walks the repeat with the scanner so its grammar is still
checked; KeepLast replaces the value in place.

write produces RFC 8259 text in one of two layouts and replaces out only on
Ok. Strings are escaped with the short forms where they exist and \u00XX for
other control characters, after UTF-8 validation that answers BadUnicode for
a string the module could not read back. Numbers use std::to_chars in its
shortest round-trip form, with .0 appended when that form has neither a
point nor an exponent, so a Number reads back as a Number. A double that is
infinite or not a number is BadNumber. write walks with a stack no deeper
than maximum_depth and answers TooDeep beyond it, so the module never
writes a document its scanner refuses. Indented is two spaces per level and
a newline after each member and item; nothing else is configurable.

# Policy and grammar

Two of the module's refusals are the grammar's and two are its own, and the
distinction is kept. A bad escape and malformed UTF-8 are outside what the
RFC admits. An unpaired surrogate escape is admitted by the grammar, which
says only that implementations handle it unpredictably; refusing it is this
module's character limit, because a decoded string here is UTF-8, in which
a lone surrogate has no encoding. Duplicate keys are recommended against and
not forbidden; rejecting them is the default and an option relaxes it.
Neither the scanner nor the writer has a lenient mode: a caller that wants
to be forgiving writes its leniency above a reader that tells the truth.

Declined, with reasons: comments and trailing commas, because a reader that
accepts more than the grammar writes documents other readers refuse, and the
hand-edited configuration format here is MDY; NaN, Infinity, and hexadecimal
numbers; streaming from a descriptor, since the caller reads the file; a
variant, templates, or a visitor, by the language document.

# The json tool

tools/json is the front end, with root launchers json and json.sh.

Opening fence
json --check FILE...      exit 0 when every file is accepted; for one that
                          is not, FILE:LINE:COLUMN: DESCRIPTION (STATUS)
                          on stderr and exit 1
json --indent FILE        the document in the Indented layout on stdout
json --compact FILE       the document in the Compact layout on stdout
json --scan FILE          one scanner token per line: offset, depth, kind,
                          bytes; stops at the scanner's first fault with the
                          line --check would print
Closing fence

Exit 1 means mm.json rejected the document, which under its policies can be
valid JSON; the message's STATUS says whether the grammar or a policy
refused it. A file that cannot be read is exit 65 and an argument fault
exit 64, as the other tools answer them. --scan reports the scanner's
faults; the two only the value layer finds, DuplicateKey and a wide
integer's BadNumber, it passes over, showing the tokens around the offset
--check names.

# Tests

tests/mm/json pins the scanner's grammar cases with their statuses,
offsets, and token sequences; the decoder, including a surrogate pair, an
overlong sequence, and a span one byte short; the numeric helpers at the
boundaries of long long and double, underflow included; the value model,
wrong-type mutation, parsing, both layouts, and the type of a number across
a round trip; every failure path's unchanged output; the depth limit in both
layers; and the JSON Parsing Test Suite under tests/mm/json/fixtures, vendored
as test data with its notice. Every y_ file parses and round-trips except
the two a documented policy rejects, y_object_duplicated_key.json and
y_object_duplicated_key_and_value.json, both DuplicateKey; every n_ file is
refused; and every i_ file has the outcome the test lists, so that a change
to one is a change someone made on purpose. tests/mm/configure runs the
tool's commands on those fixtures.

# Current boundaries

There is no write into a caller's span for a target that reports state with
no heap; the scanner's unescape has the shape it would take. There is no
extraction command in the tool; the first script consumer will shape it.
Whether a target application should ever hold a Value is for the first such
consumer to say; the scanner is what this document recommends there.
